WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.342
Think of a view as a contract.

00:00:02.342 --> 00:00:08.072
The object name matters. The exposed columns and types matter.

00:00:08.072 --> 00:00:14.287
The row grain matters. And consumers should understand how fresh the data is.

00:00:14.287 --> 00:00:22.102
If you change a shared view in a way that breaks one of these expectations,

00:00:22.102 --> 00:00:23.817
downstream queries can fail or change meaning.

00:00:23.817 --> 00:00:32.482
A standard view stores a query definition, not a separate copy of the rows.

00:00:32.482 --> 00:00:38.882
Treat the selected columns, names, types, and grain as a contract with downstream users.

00:00:38.882 --> 00:00:46.720
Changing that contract can break dashboards or applications even if the underlying query still runs.
